 "notes": "Libretto by Luigi Illica and Giuseppe Giacosa (1896). Words follow the Ricordi vocal score's spelling and punctuation (perchè; Mimì's crêma with its printed circumflex; vo'abbondanza as one word, as printed; the print's runs of dots after accanto, spazio, compagnia, perchè, poesia, fior, l'amor and Parpignol). Colline's Digna est intrari and accessit and Schaunard's Ingrediat si necessit are mock Latin, printed in italics and given in Italian-style Latin pronunciation. The chorus (girls and students, the children, the mothers, the crying boy) sings in the score and is heard with the piano; only the soloists' lines are given here: the five friends, Mimì and Parpignol, the toy seller. IPA follows sung Italian conventions: stressed syllable marked, words sung on one note joined with ‿, open e/o as ɛ/ɔ, intervocalic s voiced. Translation literal, for meaning.",
